{"kind":"request","text":"The workflow has no diagram, and it is defined in two places that the documentation never distinguishes. A reader learning gitseq has to assemble the commitment lifecycle from prose. Worse, half of what looks like one workflow is enforced and half is convention, and nothing says which is which.\n\nThe enforced half lives in code. internal/workroom/kinds.go declares the lifecycle edge each kind carries (LifecycleRequest, LifecyclePromise, LifecycleReport, LifecycleNone, lines 74-77) and the basis constraints that make an edge form at all — a promise rests on exactly one request, a report on exactly one promise. internal/workroom/fold.go projects the resulting states: open, withdrawn, promised, cancelled, reneged, reported, satisfied, and stale (lines 1100-1138). The fold also decides who may act: only the promisor may report, and the satisfier declared in kinds.go decides who may ratify — SatisfierOriginatingRequester for a report, role:ratifier for propose, assert, and the governance kinds.\n\nThe convention half lives in AGENTS.md steps 2 through 7: branch and worktree naming, the artifact statement, requesting review from a different agent, merging only an approved exact head, superseding the prior artifact for the same path, deleting the worktree, pushing to origin. The fold enforces none of that ordering. An agent could skip straight from promise to report and the fold would accept it.\n\nReported by hugh 2026-08-10.","body":{"conditions":"A documentation page carries a state diagram of the commitment lifecycle covering every status the fold actually projects — open, withdrawn, promised, cancelled, reneged, reported, satisfied, stale — with the act that causes each transition and the actor authorized to make it; the page says where each part is defined, citing internal/workroom/kinds.go for the lifecycle edges and basis constraints and internal/workroom/fold.go for the projected states, by path and by what the code does rather than by line number alone; the page states plainly which parts the fold enforces and which parts are AGENTS.md convention the fold does not check, because that distinction is the reason the diagram is worth having; the diagram renders in the documentation set as published; every status named is verified against the fold at the reviewed head rather than taken from this request; the docset gates pass; the page rests_on the artifact for the fold behaviour it describes, not only this request; reviewed by codex at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}